\title{An Explicit Bijection Between Partitions of $n$ and Conjugacy Classes of the Symmetric Group $S_n$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 26, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We present a fully formalized, constructive proof that for every natural number $n$ the partitions of $n$ are in explicit one-to-one correspondence with the conjugacy classes of the symmetric group $S_n = \Sym(\{1,\dots,n\})$. The bijection is concrete: a partition $p$ of $n$ is sent to the conjugacy class of a standard permutation $\pi_p$ whose disjoint-cycle lengths realize the parts of $p$ (parts equal to $1$ becoming fixed points), and a conjugacy class is sent to the cycle-length partition of any of its representatives. The construction is delicate because the standard \emph{cycle type} of a permutation records only cycles of length $\ge 2$, suppressing fixed points; the central lemma reconstructs the full partition by restoring exactly the right number of unit parts. Combining injectivity and surjectivity yields the equivalence $\Par(n) \simeq \Conj(S_n)$, and as an immediate corollary the number of conjugacy classes of $S_n$ equals the partition number $p(n)$, with $p(3)=3$, $p(4)=5$, $p(5)=7$. Because the number of irreducible complex characters of a finite group equals its number of conjugacy classes, this fixes the side length of the (square) character table of $S_n$ at $p(n)$.
\end{abstract}

